/*! \page developer_backend_device_page Device Arrays

\ref developer_backend_own_page      (Prev)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
\ref developer_backend_complex_page  (Next)  &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;
<BR>

In code that uses a GPU, arrays may be allocated either in the main
memory of the CPU host or in the global memory of the GPU device. 
Arrays that are allocated in GPU memory when using a CUDA backend are 
declared in PSCF as instances of specializations of a class template 
named Pscf::DeviceArray. 

The class template Pscf::DeviceArray takes two template parameters, and 
is declared as:
\code
   template <typename Data, class T> class DeviceArray
\endcode
Template parameter Data is the type of each element in the array, while
parameter T is a backend identifer class. Partial specializations of the 
DeviceArray template with T=Pscf::CUT and T=Pscf::CPT are defined to be 
used with a CUDA GPU backend or a C++ CPU backend, respectively.

The use of a backend identifier class as a template parameter allows the 
DeviceArray template to be used within definitions of other class templates
that also take T as a template parameter. In such templates, an instance of
Pscf::DeviceArray<Data,T> represents an array that is allocated allocated 
in GPU memory when it appears in a template specialization that uses a 
CUDA backend (with T=Pscf::CUT) but that is allocated in CPU heap memory 
in specializations that use a C++ backend (with T=Pscf::CPT). 

Partial specializations of DeviceArray that are designed for use with
different backents are discussed below.

<b> CUDA backend (CUT) </b>:

The partial specialization of DeviceArray with T=Pscf::CUT is a wrapper
for an underlying array that is allocated in global GPU device memory,
and is defined in the file src/pscf/backend/cuda/DeviceArray.h.  For
example, the specialization Pscf::DeviceArray<double,Pscf::CUT> is thus 
an array of double precision numbers that is allocated in GPU memory.  

Specializations of DeviceArray<Data,T> with T=Pscf::CUT do not allow 
access to individual elements of the array from the CPU host, and 
instead only allow array elements to be accessed within CUDA kernels. 
Mathematical operations on such arrays are thus always performed by 
functions that launch CUDA kernels.

<b> C++ backend (CPT) </b>:

The partial specialization of DeviceArray with T=Pscf::CPT is a wrapper 
for a underlying array that is allocated in CPU host memory, and is 
defined in the file src/pscf/backend/cpp/DeviceArray.h.  For example, 
an instance of class DeviceArray<double,Pscf::CPT> is thus an array of 
double precision numbers that is allocated in CPU memory. Such an array
is similar in many respects to an instance of DArray<double>.  One 
feature of the DeviceArray<Data,Pscf::CPT> template that distinguishes 
it from DArray<Data>, is that a DeviceArray<Data,Pscf::CPT> allocates 
memory using functions provided by the FFTW fast Fourier transform 
library, which align memory in a manner that optimizes the performance 
of that library. 

Each specialization of DeviceArray<Data,T> with T=CPT is derived from
a corresponding specialization of the template Util::Array<Data>. The 
Util::Array template defines a subscript operator that provides access 
to individual array elements within code that runs on the CPU host.

<BR>
